            0.15
          ],
          "content": "\\_ Math Talk一"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.047,
            0.419,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.088,
            0.563,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Recreate this on an isometric grid."
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.113,
            0.509,
            0.134
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Why does the illusion work?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.163,
            0.59,
            0.189
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "SUMMARY"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.211,
            0.821,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": "Fractals are self-similar geometric objects found in nature and in art."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.259,
            0.822,
            0.337
          ],
          "content": "The Sierpinski Carpet, Sierpinski Gasket, and Koch Snowflake are some examples of mathematical fractals. They can be obtained by repeatedly applying certain geometric operations that generate a sequence of shapes approaching the fractal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.344,
            0.823,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": "Cuboids, tetrahedrons, cylinders, cones, prisms, pyramids, and octahedrons are some of the solids that can be obtained by folding suitable **nets**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.411,
            0.821,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": "The shortest path between two points on the surface of a cuboid can be found by using a suitable net of the cuboid."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.459,
            0.822,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": "Any object can be represented on a plane surface by using its **projections** on plane surfaces. For this purpose, we generally use the **front view** (projection on the **vertical plane**), **top view** (projection on the **horizontal plane**), and **side view** (projection on the **side plane**) of the object."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.562,
            0.822,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "A cube can be oriented such that the lengths of all its edges in the projection are equal. Such a projection is called the **isometric projection**. Isometric projections of different solids can be drawn using isometric grid paper."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.082,
            0.913,
            0.129,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
